29
00:01:57,602 --> 00:02:00,922
Antoni Gaudí was a Catalan architect who worked in Barcelona around

30
00:02:00,922 --> 00:02:04,242
the turn of 20th century from the 1880s up until his death.

31
00:02:05,474 --> 00:02:08,076
in 1926. You probably know his work,

32
00:02:08,076 --> 00:02:10,609
you know, the buildings like the Sagrada Família,

33
00:02:10,609 --> 00:02:14,792
Casa Batlló, Casa Milà, and Park Güell are some of

34
00:02:14,792 --> 00:02:17,514
the most recognizable architectural pieces in the world.

35
00:02:17,514 --> 00:02:20,447
Here the Sagrada Família, here Batlló,

36
00:02:20,447 --> 00:02:23,749
Milà, and Güell.

37
00:02:23,749 --> 00:02:28,693
He took over the Sagrada Família in 1883 and ended up devoting the rest

38
00:02:28,693 --> 00:02:33,294
of his life to it, working on it right until he died.

39
00:02:34,518 --> 00:02:38,130
What I think his buildings are known for are his organic forms,

40
00:02:38,130 --> 00:02:44,383
the flowing, the shapes, these things that architects aren't easily able

41
00:02:44,383 --> 00:02:47,925
to draw and let alone that it's really hard to build these things.

42
00:02:47,925 --> 00:02:50,657
But I think it's good that when we talk about Gaudí,

43
00:02:50,657 --> 00:02:52,708
we don't really talk about how his buildings look,

44
00:02:52,708 --> 00:02:58,431
but we focus on the way the buildings work and specifically about

45
00:02:58,431 --> 00:03:02,073
how he integrated tools into his design process to create these buildings.

46
00:03:04,374 --> 00:03:09,896
Church of Colònia Güell Gaudí built a hanging chain model and he spent close

47
00:03:09,896 --> 00:03:14,947
to a decade refining it. Important thing is to understand this is it's

48
00:03:14,947 --> 00:03:18,878
not a sketch or drawing in a way,

49
00:03:18,878 --> 00:03:22,379
it's a physical calculator that helped him build the building.

50
00:03:22,379 --> 00:03:24,339
Because when you hang a chain,

51
00:03:24,339 --> 00:03:27,840
you can imagine with multiple weights at certain points,

52
00:03:27,840 --> 00:03:31,862
you craft a shape that's able to carry itself on,

53
00:03:31,862 --> 00:03:33,292
to carry its own.

54
00:03:33,504 --> 00:03:36,695
It's built on pure tension so that when you reverse it,

55
00:03:36,695 --> 00:03:41,658
you flip it upside down, you get one form that stands in pure compression,

56
00:03:41,658 --> 00:03:45,980
which is exactly what masonry and stone are great

57
00:03:46,260 --> 00:03:51,262
at dealing with compression. So in a real sense,

58
00:03:51,262 --> 00:03:55,764
Gaudí was using gravity to resolve a structural problem for him,

59
00:03:55,764 --> 00:03:59,746
a structural problem he probably wasn't able to solve.

60
00:04:00,086 --> 00:04:02,587
or to calculate in a way, but he was able to,

61
00:04:02,587 --> 00:04:08,260
let's say, simplify it, translate it as abstracted in a way through,

62
00:04:08,260 --> 00:04:14,492
say, using a model. And that's also how we should really think about Gaudí.

63
00:04:14,492 --> 00:04:19,043
He's not really somebody that trusted or used drawings or abstraction,

64
00:04:19,043 --> 00:04:22,806
though he preferred to work through physical models because they

65
00:04:22,806 --> 00:04:27,538
let him understand space directly rather than

66
00:04:27,694 --> 00:04:29,894
Let's say creating an abstract representation,

67
00:04:29,894 --> 00:04:33,337
a drawing, which then would have you interpret space through that,

68
00:04:33,337 --> 00:04:38,280
a rendering, right? His entire design process ran through the tools he used,

69
00:04:38,280 --> 00:04:40,981
not through the drawings of the final result.

70
00:04:40,981 --> 00:04:47,505
In other words, Gaudí wasn't just producing a building,

71
00:04:47,505 --> 00:04:51,647
was producing a tool that would help him shape a building.

72
00:04:51,647 --> 00:04:56,790
And I think that's the part that really matters for what we're doing.

73
00:04:57,102 --> 00:04:59,982
Now these curves aren't a stylistic decision.

74
00:04:59,982 --> 00:05:06,102
Well, they are, but they're a result of a design process where

75
00:05:06,102 --> 00:05:11,302
the direct output of a structural system results in its efficiency,

76
00:05:11,302 --> 00:05:17,202
right? The efficiency results in the shape and that shape is a,

77
00:05:17,202 --> 00:05:19,142
is an answer to a physical problem.

78
00:05:19,142 --> 00:05:24,942
So when, when Thomas is going to be explaining that Gaudí workflow,

79
00:05:24,942 --> 00:05:26,862
when you're looking

80
00:05:28,938 --> 00:05:33,319
To train an AI model, let's say more traditionally with images,

81
00:05:33,319 --> 00:05:38,581
you will get things like curves and undulating surfaces and texture in material.

82
00:05:38,581 --> 00:05:41,972
But what you won't get is the conceptual transfer.

83
00:05:41,972 --> 00:05:43,282
So you'll get a stylistic transfer.

84
00:05:43,282 --> 00:05:46,213
You won't get a conceptual transfer,

85
00:05:46,213 --> 00:05:51,595
right? And between the copying of the appearance and understanding

86
00:05:51,595 --> 00:05:54,205
the underlying logic, there will be a gap.

87
00:05:54,205 --> 00:05:56,216
And that's the one we need to close.

88
00:05:57,382 --> 00:05:59,863
And that's the whole point of this module.

89
00:05:59,863 --> 00:06:04,196
So none of the work that you are going to be doing should be about copying Gaudí's

90
00:06:04,196 --> 00:06:08,989
aesthetic, or making something that just looks like Gaudí. It should not be that.

91
00:06:08,989 --> 00:06:15,872
It's about using AI as a tool to let us understand structural logic

92
00:06:15,973 --> 00:06:19,915
and form instead of drawing a shape and hoping it stands the test of time.

93
